e0$. In the source's dual convention this is Theorem 3.39: $D^{\lambda}
e0$ iff $\lambda$ is $e$-restricted. That proof has two halves. (a) If $\lambda$ is $e$-restricted, the $e$-ladder tableau of Lemma 3.37 is $e$-restricted and Lemma 3.35 produces at least one composition factor of $S^{\lambda}$ carried by the interpolated $\mathbb{L}$-modules, so $D^{\lambda}
e0$. (b) If $\lambda$ is not $e$-restricted then $[e]_q$ divides the common factor $g^{\lambda}$ of every Gram entry in Lemma 3.38, whence $[e]_q=0$ forces every Gram entry $\langle m_s,m_tangle_\lambda$ to vanish, so the cell form is identically zero, $\mathrm{rad}\,S^{\lambda}=S^{\lambda}$ and $D^{\lambda}=0$. Translating (a),(b) through the signed conjugate Murphy-cell dictionary of [[def-type-a-hecke-specht-module-and-cell-form]] replaces $e$-restricted by $e$-regular and twists every module by the same involutive automorphism $	heta$, preserving simplicity and quotients. Part (3) is Corollary 3.40 of the source ($e>n$ iff split semisimple). No choice is used.

